      "writtenOn": "2023-11-03T01:11:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "binder_get_user_page_remote() is where we actually insert the page and do the smb_store_release() that is checked at line 272. Since the insertion of the page is guaranteed to occur after line 272, Is the race only possible for the head/tail pages which might be shared with another transaction? So the load.acq is used from Thread A in case Thread B is in the middle of setting up that page (finished with store.rel)?\n\nIt looks like we can lose that race and load.acq returns NULL at line 272, but  Thread B might do the store.rel before we get to this line. In this case the check at line 220 prevents us from allocating again - and it holds the lock so it gets a non-racy answer. The benefit of the check at line 272 is really an optimization to avoid acquiring mmap_write_lock when no alloc is needed.\n\nGiven this optimization is racy anyway, do we really need the acq/rel semantics? Does it make a difference? We don\u0027t have to worry about page going from a valid pointer to NULL.\n\nDo I understand this correctly? Add lots of comments about this (especially if I still don\u0027t understand it ;-).",
      "revId": "cba0dd82a60df879b05f8887eacf6faf78ab002b",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
